      "label": "Watermarked",
      "description": "Original text with KGW watermark embedded.",
      "text": "Explain what a zero-knowledge proof is and give a simple analogy. A zero-knowledge proof is a cryptographic method where one party can prove to another that they know a value without revealing the value itself. Think of it like a blindfolded maze solver: you can convince someone you know the path through the maze by walking it correctly every time they ask, without ever showing them the actual path. The verifier learns nothing about the solution, only that you possess it. This technique has profound applications in blockchain privacy, authentication systems, and secure voting protocols.",
